To check whether your local folder and GitHub repository are completely in sync, you need to verify two things: if you have uncommitted changes locally, and if your local branch matches the latest updates on GitHub.

1. Check for local uncommitted changes
Before checking GitHub, see if you have any modified, deleted, or new files in your local folder that haven't been committed yet. 
Run: git status

• If in sync locally: It will say "nothing to commit, working tree clean".
• If out of sync locally: It will list untracked or modified files that need to be added (git add) and committed (git commit).

2. Compare your local folder with GitHub
Git does not automatically look at GitHub in real-time; it uses a local cache of the remote repository. 
You must download the latest metadata from GitHub first to compare them.
Run the following commands (replace main with your branch name if it's different):
bash 
# 1. Fetch the latest updates from GitHub without changing your files
git fetch origin

# 2. Check the status comparison
git status

Understanding the git status output:

• Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.
🎉 Everything is in sync. Your local folder and GitHub match perfectly.
• Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by X commits.
⚠️ GitHub is behind. You have committed changes locally that you haven't pushed to GitHub yet. (Fix by running git push origin main).
• Your branch is behind 'origin/main' by X commits.
⚠️ Your local folder is behind. Someone else (or you, via the web interface) pushed updates to GitHub that you don't have yet. (Fix by running git pull origin main).
• Your branch and 'origin/main' have diverged.
⚠️ Both have different changes. You have new commits locally, and there are also new commits on GitHub. You will need to run git pull and resolve any merge conflicts.

3. See exactly what files are different
If git status tells you that the branches are not in sync, you can view the exact line-by-line file differences before syncing them:
bash git diff main origin/main

